<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaan Poska</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaan Poska was an Estonian lawyer and statesman, mayor of Reval (Tallinn), who became commissar of the Autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> created in 1917 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a>. He later served in the provisional government of the Republic of Estonia, and as head of the Estonian delegation he signed the Treaty of Tartu with Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1920, which recognised Estonian independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaan-poska.jpg" alt="Jaan Poska" width="320" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virumaa_Muuseumid_RM_F_1358_1,_Jaan_Poska.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jaan Poska was born on 24 January 1866 at Laiusevälja, near Jõgeva, the fifth of 12 children of an Eastern Orthodox parish schoolmaster. He received his secondary education at the church's school in Riga and entered medical school at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tartu">University of Tartu</a>, but soon moved to law and graduated in 1890. He was the first native Estonian speaker admitted to the bar in Tallinn, where he worked as a barrister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As mayor of Tallinn from 1913 to 1917 Poska supported reforms in healthcare and the founding of two schools. In April 1917 he was put at the head of the Autonomous Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. On 28 November 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-provincial-assembly">Maapäev</a> refused to recognise the new Bolshevik rule and proclaimed itself the supreme legal authority of Estonia, and the Republic of Estonia declared independence on 24 February 1918, only to be occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in November 1918.</p><p>Poska was appointed Minister of Foreign Affairs on 24 February 1918. He worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> to win diplomatic recognition for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>. He then led the peace talks with Soviet Russia that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a>, and the Treaty of Tartu was signed on 2 February 1920. He also helped to write the first Estonian constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Poska died unexpectedly in Tallinn on 7 March 1920, aged 54, only weeks after the treaty was signed. He was the first Estonian ever to be given a state funeral, and over 20,000 people attended it. His daughter Vera Poska-Grünthal became a leading Estonian feminist and lawyer and married the politician and lawyer Timotheus Grünthal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jaan Poska” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jaan Poska</b>, mayor of Reval, became its commissar.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-governorate-of-estonia">Autonomous Governorate of Estonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Laiuse Rural Municipality. Died in Tallinn, in what is now <a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.44,24.75&amp;year=1901" aria-label="Tallinn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Jaan Poska"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4772 5039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4772 5039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=59.44,24.75&amp;year=1901">What was here in 1901? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
